46
00:02:26,180 --> 00:02:27,820
as a sequence of information.

47
00:02:27,820 --> 00:02:32,340
And we want to make a prediction about the state of the system.

48
00:02:32,340 --> 00:02:35,100
And so beyond that simple example, sequential data

49
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:38,580
as Alexander mentioned is truly all around us, right?

50
00:02:38,580 --> 00:02:42,180
As I'm speaking and I'm projecting these audio waveforms,

51
00:02:42,180 --> 00:02:44,500
that is a sequence that can be split up

52
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:47,820
into a series of chunks of sound waves.

53
00:02:47,820 --> 00:02:50,740
Similarly with text, we can think about

54
00:02:50,740 --> 00:02:53,340
breaking that text up into a sequence

55
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:58,140
of individual characters or individual words.

56
00:02:58,140 --> 00:03:01,020
And beyond these examples, sequential data

57
00:03:01,020 --> 00:03:02,980
is really, really all around us,

58
00:03:02,980 --> 00:03:06,620
from medical signals to financial time series,

59
00:03:06,620 --> 00:03:11,140
like modeling the stark market, to biology and chemistry

60
00:03:11,140 --> 00:03:14,580
and the life sciences and thinking about biological sequences

61
00:03:14,580 --> 00:03:17,340
and modalities, to patterns of movement,

62
00:03:17,340 --> 00:03:20,660
to videos, to weather, all around us

63
00:03:20,660 --> 00:03:22,660
there are these problems that can be thought of

64
00:03:22,660 --> 00:03:24,940
from a sequential modeling perspective.

65
00:03:25,940 --> 00:03:28,500
And so that gives us a concrete notion

66
00:03:28,500 --> 00:03:31,740
of how we can bring this idea of sequence modeling

67
00:03:31,740 --> 00:03:33,320
to the real world.

68
00:03:33,320 --> 00:03:36,980
But what are some tasks that we now could think about

69
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:41,740
training neural networks to facilitate in this context?

70
00:03:41,740 --> 00:03:45,060
In lecture one, we talked about this simple example

71
00:03:45,060 --> 00:03:47,780
of classification, binary classification.

72
00:03:47,780 --> 00:03:51,180
Yes or no, will I pass this class,

73
00:03:51,180 --> 00:03:54,940
given a student as input, produce a zero-one prediction

74
00:03:54,940 --> 00:03:57,180
about pass versus fail?

75
00:03:57,180 --> 00:04:00,940
We can also do similar classification type problems

76
00:04:00,940 --> 00:04:03,660
or tasks with sequences, right?

77
00:04:03,660 --> 00:04:07,700
We can think about taking a body of text, let's say a tweet,

78
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:10,700
and assigning a emotion or a sentiment

79
00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:14,580
as a class label to that text.

80
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:16,660
On the other hand, we could go rather

81
00:04:16,660 --> 00:04:19,100
from sequence to one output.

82
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:23,020
We could have one input and produce a sequence of output.

83
00:04:23,020 --> 00:04:25,780
So say in the image captioning world

84
00:04:25,780 --> 00:04:29,220
or the vision language world, where given an image,

85
00:04:29,220 --> 00:04:35,660
we want to train a neural network to produce text describing the content of that image that

86
00:04:35,660 --> 00:04:39,820
also falls into this paradigm of sequence modeling.

87
00:04:39,820 --> 00:04:44,780
And also we can think about many inputs going to many outputs.

88
00:04:44,780 --> 00:04:50,780
So if you want to translate text or audio from one language to another, we have to do this

89
00:04:50,780 --> 00:04:58,780
transformation of learning a function to map English text to, let's say, text in Mandarin.

90
00:04:58,780 --> 00:05:07,020
So these are some three examples of core types of modeling tasks we can bring to the sequence

91
00:05:07,020 --> 00:05:08,700
modeling world.

92
00:05:08,700 --> 00:05:15,180
So today we're going to think about these types of problems and we're going to build up intuition

93
00:05:15,180 --> 00:05:20,700
about how we can actually define different types of neural network architectures to tackle

94
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:27,420
the unique features of these types of sequence modeling problems.

95
00:05:27,420 --> 00:05:34,300
So the first core part of this lecture is going to be around this idea of building neurons

96
00:05:34,300 --> 00:05:40,860
that have a notion of recurrence that allows them to process time resolved or sequential

97
00:05:40,860 --> 00:05:43,600
data one step at a time.

98
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:48,820
And I think this topic is something that when I was first learning machine learning and

99
00:05:48,820 --> 00:05:53,500
deep learning was something I myself had a struggle to wrap my head around.

100
00:05:53,500 --> 00:06:00,300
And so what we've tried to do with this next block of content is really build a fundamental

101
00:06:00,300 --> 00:06:07,020
intuition about how this concept works, building from the ground up and connecting back to

102
00:06:07,020 --> 00:06:13,740
what Alexander introduced with feed-forward models to actually build up that intuition effectively.

103
00:06:13,740 --> 00:06:17,820
So to do that, let's first go back to the perceptron, right?

104
00:06:17,820 --> 00:06:25,130
building block of neural networks and go step by step to the sequence modeling world.

105
00:06:25,130 --> 00:06:29,210
When we looked at the perceptron, we had this kind of diagram, right?

106
00:06:29,210 --> 00:06:36,090
This function where we have a set of inputs, let's call them x1 through xm, and we have

107
00:06:36,090 --> 00:06:43,650
an individual perceptron unit that takes these inputs, multiplies each of them by a weight,

108
00:06:43,650 --> 00:06:51,850
non-linearity, non-linear activation function to produce an output Y-Hab.

109
00:06:51,850 --> 00:06:58,250
And so we saw that we can have multiple inputs going in to this perceptron, and you can

110
00:06:58,250 --> 00:07:02,050
think of these as inputs at a particular time.

111
00:07:02,050 --> 00:07:08,730
We also saw how we can stack these individual perceptrons together to build out a layer

112
00:07:08,730 --> 00:07:10,730
of a neural network.

113
00:07:10,730 --> 00:07:15,810
So what you're seeing here is that extension, right, where in green we have this layer of

114
00:07:15,810 --> 00:07:24,250
individual perceptrons where we can map multi-dimensional input to a multi-dimensional output.

115
00:07:24,250 --> 00:07:31,010
But so far even though we have a multi-dimensional input, does this have a notion of time steps

116
00:07:31,010 --> 00:07:32,490
or of sequence?

117
00:07:32,490 --> 00:07:34,530
No, not yet.

118
00:07:34,530 --> 00:07:40,690
And so to get there, let's take this diagram and collapse it down and simplify it a bit.

119
00:07:40,690 --> 00:07:46,690
So all I'm showing you here is that inner layer of the stack of perceptrons collapsed

120
00:07:46,690 --> 00:07:51,370
into this layer-wise block, right, shown here in green.

121
00:07:51,370 --> 00:07:59,210
And our inputs are going to be x of t, x at some time step t, and they're going to be

122
00:07:59,210 --> 00:08:04,490
fed into this layer and producing an output y hat.

123
00:08:04,490 --> 00:08:08,890
But we're still just considering the input at a particular time step.

124
00:08:08,890 --> 00:08:12,570
and we're denoting that by the marker, little t.

125
00:08:12,570 --> 00:08:15,230
This is nothing different than what we saw

126
00:08:15,230 --> 00:08:16,570
in the first lecture, right?

127
00:08:16,570 --> 00:08:20,130
We're just beginning to get this notion

128
00:08:20,130 --> 00:08:22,210
that this is processing information

129
00:08:22,210 --> 00:08:23,570
from just one time step.

130
00:08:24,770 --> 00:08:27,510
Now, let's flip this on its side.

131
00:08:27,510 --> 00:08:30,570
So we've just rotated this 90 degrees,

132
00:08:30,570 --> 00:08:32,690
in perfector going into the layer,

133
00:08:32,690 --> 00:08:34,290
getting our output vector.

134
00:08:35,250 --> 00:08:38,850
What if we had this sequential data over time?

135
00:08:38,850 --> 00:08:41,890
And let's say we took our same feet forward model

136
00:08:41,890 --> 00:08:45,330
and tried to apply it time step by time step

137
00:08:45,330 --> 00:08:47,450
over and over again.

138
00:08:47,450 --> 00:08:50,210
If we did that, we would see something like this

139
00:08:50,210 --> 00:08:55,050
where at time step zero, we take our input,

140
00:08:55,050 --> 00:08:57,690
pass it through our no network layer,

141
00:08:57,690 --> 00:09:00,210
produce an output y hat.

142
00:09:00,210 --> 00:09:03,130
We did this at time step one, do the same thing,

143
00:09:03,130 --> 00:09:05,890
pass in the input, produce an output,

144
00:09:05,890 --> 00:09:09,210
at time step two, so on.

145
00:09:09,210 --> 00:09:12,850
And even if we did this time step to time step,

146
00:09:12,850 --> 00:09:15,650
we'd be applying our neural network function

147
00:09:15,650 --> 00:09:18,650
to the inputs and predicting outputs.

148
00:09:18,650 --> 00:09:20,690
But still, they're not connected at all, right?

149
00:09:20,690 --> 00:09:23,090
These time steps are isolated.

150
00:09:23,090 --> 00:09:26,390
We're just treating x of 0 at time step 0,

151
00:09:26,390 --> 00:09:30,610
x of 1, at time step 1, so on and so forth.

152
00:09:30,610 --> 00:09:34,130
But really the core thing with sequential data

153
00:09:34,130 --> 00:09:40,970
is that there is some relation between the inputs at time step t to the inputs at time

154
00:09:40,970 --> 00:09:45,370
step t minus 1, t minus 2, and so on and so forth.

155
00:09:45,370 --> 00:09:47,530
Just like the example with the ball.

156
00:09:47,530 --> 00:09:52,690
If we treat the time steps in isolation, we have no information about the past trajectory

157
00:09:52,690 --> 00:09:53,890
of the ball.

158
00:09:53,890 --> 00:09:59,250
But if we connect the time steps together, then we can use that past history to make a better

159
00:09:59,250 --> 00:10:00,010
prediction

160
00:10:00,010 --> 00:10:04,160
about the current state or the future state.

161
00:10:04,160 --> 00:10:10,280
And so this to concretize this a little more, what that is saying is that the predicted

162
00:10:10,280 --> 00:10:18,360
output at a later time step, say T equals 2, should depend somehow on the inputs at the

163
00:10:18,360 --> 00:10:23,880
prior time steps, say x of 1, x of 0.

164
00:10:23,880 --> 00:10:33,560
And so we want a way to preserve this information and carry it forward to make predictions at later time steps.

165
00:10:33,560 --> 00:10:45,480
And the idea behind doing this is to link and relate the network's computations at the particular time step to the past history of the prior time steps.

166
00:10:45,480 --> 00:10:49,560
So let's try by doing exactly that.

167
00:10:49,560 --> 00:10:58,060
What this diagram is now showing is by connecting these outputs and the states at these past

168
00:10:58,060 --> 00:11:04,760
time steps, we're linking the computation of the network across time.

169
00:11:04,760 --> 00:11:11,240
Specifically we can introduce this notion of an internal state of the network.

170
00:11:11,240 --> 00:11:18,320
And we're going to denote this using the variable H, where this is going to be actually maintained

171
00:11:18,320 --> 00:11:23,760
by the neural network itself and pass on time step to time step.

172
00:11:23,760 --> 00:11:30,040
So we get this idea of sort of memory of this internal state of the neural network that's

173
00:11:30,040 --> 00:11:38,960
going to depend not only on the past computations but also the input at that time step.

174
00:11:38,960 --> 00:11:45,860
What this means very concretely is when the network makes a prediction at time step

175
00:11:45,860 --> 00:11:52,860
that's going to be a function of not only the input at that time step,

176
00:11:52,860 --> 00:11:59,860
but also the past memory or the state that's captured in this variable of state,

177
00:11:59,860 --> 00:12:03,860
H of T minus 1. So that past memory.

178
00:12:03,860 --> 00:12:06,860
And you can see that concretely in this example in the middle,

179
00:12:06,860 --> 00:12:11,860
where prediction at time step 1 depends on input at time step 1,

180
00:12:11,860 --> 00:12:16,420
step one, as well as the past state.

181
00:12:16,420 --> 00:12:21,740
And so this is the score idea of what we think of as recurrence.

182
00:12:21,740 --> 00:12:25,940
Meaning simply, how can we link in just one network, right?

183
00:12:25,940 --> 00:12:31,020
The network is not, you know, we're not creating new networks

184
00:12:31,020 --> 00:12:31,820
per time set.

185
00:12:31,820 --> 00:12:33,620
It's still one network.

186
00:12:33,620 --> 00:12:37,620
But we're connecting the information from the past computations

187
00:12:37,620 --> 00:12:41,540
to the predictions at a particular time set.

188
00:12:41,540 --> 00:12:44,180
And so because this output, this prediction,

189
00:12:44,180 --> 00:12:48,500
is a function of both the current input and the past memory,

190
00:12:48,500 --> 00:12:53,260
we describe these neural networks via this recurrence relation.

191
00:12:53,260 --> 00:12:56,660
And so you can show this on the left here

192
00:12:56,660 --> 00:12:59,940
where we have this notion of a cycle, this looping,

193
00:12:59,940 --> 00:13:03,440
which shows that h of t is being recurrently

194
00:13:03,440 --> 00:13:07,060
provided to the network at these different time steps

195
00:13:07,060 --> 00:13:09,060
to make the predictions.

196
00:13:09,060 --> 00:13:12,000
And on the right, we just see that kind of rolled out

197
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:13,220
over these time steps.

198
00:13:13,220 --> 00:13:17,360
It's in a way to me, it's a clearer visualization

199
00:13:17,360 --> 00:13:19,120
of what is actually going on.

200
00:13:20,460 --> 00:13:24,760
And so this core idea of maintaining an internal state,

201
00:13:24,760 --> 00:13:28,700
HFT, updating it per individual time steps

202
00:13:28,700 --> 00:13:32,380
and using that to inform the predicted output

203
00:13:32,380 --> 00:13:37,100
is the core intuitive idea behind a very large class

204
00:13:37,100 --> 00:13:42,100
of neural networks called recurrent neural networks or RNNs.

205
00:13:42,860 --> 00:13:45,140
And this is really the key to understanding

206
00:13:45,140 --> 00:13:47,820
these frameworks of RNNs.

207
00:13:47,820 --> 00:13:49,940
So we're going to continue to build up

208
00:13:49,940 --> 00:13:53,540
from this foundation to gain more and more understanding

209
00:13:53,540 --> 00:13:56,340
about the behavior of these neural networks.

210
00:13:57,420 --> 00:14:00,220
So let's formalize this a bit more.

211
00:14:00,220 --> 00:14:03,740
Right, as we saw, this core idea is to maintain

212
00:14:03,740 --> 00:14:10,460
internal state, updated at each time set that this as the sequence is processed dynamically.

213
00:14:11,260 --> 00:14:17,740
And so the way we do this is via this recurrence relation, which defines how that update actually

214
00:14:17,740 --> 00:14:26,940
occurs per unit time. Making this more concrete, this notion of this cell state, H of T,

215
00:14:26,940 --> 00:14:33,940
is going to be a function parameterized by a set of weights, just like with Fienette Forward Networks,

216
00:14:33,940 --> 00:14:38,940
just like with any network we're going to see throughout this course, right?

217
00:14:38,940 --> 00:14:43,940
We always define these updates according to these learnable sets of weights,

218
00:14:43,940 --> 00:14:51,940
and that state update depends on the current input and the prior state from the prior time step.

219
00:14:51,940 --> 00:14:53,440
Right?

220
00:14:53,440 --> 00:14:58,900
Cell state is a function defined by a set of weights w that

221
00:14:58,900 --> 00:15:03,340
depends on the input and the old state.

222
00:15:03,340 --> 00:15:07,020
And with an RNN type model, we use that same function

223
00:15:07,020 --> 00:15:10,260
in set of parameters at every time step.

224
00:15:10,260 --> 00:15:13,140
And when we actually train the model, as we'll see,

225
00:15:13,140 --> 00:15:18,180
those weights get updated per training step.

226
00:15:18,180 --> 00:15:21,780
So another way to get more intuition from this

227
00:15:21,780 --> 00:15:25,220
is also to look at an example in pseudocode.

228
00:15:25,220 --> 00:15:27,820
So let's go through that through the algorithm

229
00:15:27,820 --> 00:15:31,140
for the state update of these state-based models

230
00:15:31,140 --> 00:15:34,820
and get a better sense of how these networks work.

231
00:15:34,820 --> 00:15:38,060
So we start by initializing the state to something,

232
00:15:38,060 --> 00:15:42,020
let's say, 0, 0, 0.

233
00:15:42,020 --> 00:15:45,860
And our prediction here is going to look at text.

234
00:15:45,860 --> 00:15:49,740
Text data, we break it up into a sequence of words.

235
00:15:49,740 --> 00:15:55,100
And our task is to predict the next word in the sentence.

236
00:15:55,100 --> 00:15:59,860
So we take our input sentence and we take our initialized RNN

237
00:15:59,860 --> 00:16:01,940
with its initialized state.

238
00:16:01,940 --> 00:16:05,980
And then we iterate time step by time step word by word

239
00:16:05,980 --> 00:16:10,540
in that sentence and call a function of the RNN

240
00:16:10,540 --> 00:16:15,300
to generate a prediction and update the hidden state per word,

241
00:16:15,300 --> 00:16:17,300
right, per time step.

242
00:16:17,300 --> 00:16:19,980
And at the end, once we've stepped through all the words

243
00:16:19,980 --> 00:16:23,580
so far in the sentence, we can get a prediction for the next

244
00:16:23,580 --> 00:16:28,060
word by taking the output of the RNN at that last time step.

245
00:16:30,060 --> 00:16:33,740
So this is really a core idea behind this idea

246
00:16:33,740 --> 00:16:35,740
of next word prediction.

247
00:16:35,740 --> 00:16:40,980
One way to approach it is by going step by step, word by word

248
00:16:40,980 --> 00:16:43,420
through a sentence and predicting the,

249
00:16:43,420 --> 00:16:48,420
and learning this relation kind of recursively over time.

250
00:16:49,660 --> 00:16:53,220
So as we kind of saw with an RNN,

251
00:16:53,220 --> 00:16:57,740
we need to update both the state and predict an output.

252
00:16:57,740 --> 00:17:02,740
So to take this to the last level of concreteness

253
00:17:03,060 --> 00:17:06,320
and bring a little bit math into this,

254
00:17:06,320 --> 00:17:09,900
our relationship here is we're given this input vector.

255
00:17:09,900 --> 00:17:12,380
We first update the hidden state

256
00:17:12,380 --> 00:17:17,060
And we're going to apply a function to do this.

257
00:17:17,060 --> 00:17:20,900
To break this down, it maybe looks a little intimidating at first

258
00:17:20,900 --> 00:17:24,100
with multiple variables here, multiple weights.

259
00:17:24,100 --> 00:17:28,180
But it's really very similar to the neural net operations

260
00:17:28,180 --> 00:17:31,740
that we saw in lecture one that Alexander introduced.

261
00:17:31,740 --> 00:17:34,620
Just like before, we have multiplications

262
00:17:34,620 --> 00:17:36,220
by weight matrices, right?

263
00:17:36,220 --> 00:17:40,620
Sets of weights that are applied to both the past hidden state

264
00:17:40,620 --> 00:17:44,260
and the input, and we take those something together

265
00:17:44,260 --> 00:17:47,020
and apply a non-linear activation function, right?

266
00:17:47,020 --> 00:17:51,020
That's the tan H hyperbolic tangent function here.

267
00:17:51,020 --> 00:17:54,420
And that gives our hidden state update.

268
00:17:54,420 --> 00:17:56,820
And to do the output prediction,

269
00:17:56,820 --> 00:18:01,660
we take another weight matrix and transform the hidden state

270
00:18:01,660 --> 00:18:03,740
and predict the output.

271
00:18:03,740 --> 00:18:07,100
And importantly, right, these three sets of weight matrices

272
00:18:07,100 --> 00:18:08,780
are learned.

273
00:18:08,780 --> 00:18:10,660
We start them, we initialize them,

274
00:18:10,660 --> 00:18:13,500
and over the course of the training of the RNN,

275
00:18:13,500 --> 00:18:16,900
we update the parameters of these weight matrices

276
00:18:16,900 --> 00:18:20,340
through gradient descent and the similar algorithms

277
00:18:20,340 --> 00:18:22,420
of training the neural network

278
00:18:22,420 --> 00:18:24,660
that we saw in lecture one.

279
00:18:26,020 --> 00:18:28,580
So putting this all together, right?

280
00:18:28,580 --> 00:18:31,580
How does this come together?

281
00:18:31,580 --> 00:18:36,260
We have our RNN with this recurrent loop-like relation,

282
00:18:36,260 --> 00:18:44,260
And we saw that we can unroll this step by step across these time steps and visualize the RNN that way.

283
00:18:44,260 --> 00:18:56,260
When we do that, we break it down from time step zero to time step one, so on and so forth, all the way up to time step T, whatever the length of the input is.

284
00:18:56,260 --> 00:19:04,260
And across all these steps, we're making these predictions at these individual time steps.

285
00:19:04,260 --> 00:19:12,420
We can make the weight matrices explicit, showing the weight matrix that maps input to the hidden state,

286
00:19:12,420 --> 00:19:17,060
the hidden state weight matrix as well as the output.

287
00:19:17,060 --> 00:19:24,290
And we reuse the same matrices that every time step.

288
00:19:24,290 --> 00:19:29,970
When we make a forward pass, a call through the model to actually generate the prediction,

289
00:19:29,970 --> 00:19:35,770
because we're generating predictions at each time step, that means we can compute a loss

290
00:19:35,770 --> 00:19:38,130
at each individual time set.

291
00:19:38,130 --> 00:19:41,690
And we can sum them together to get an overall loss

292
00:19:41,690 --> 00:19:44,770
that gives us the whole predictive loss

293
00:19:44,770 --> 00:19:47,850
over the entire sequence.

294
00:19:47,850 --> 00:19:50,410
So that gives us a broken down way

295
00:19:50,410 --> 00:19:54,450
of how this RNN forward pass, a call to the model,

296
00:19:54,450 --> 00:19:56,810
actually occurs in practice.

297
00:19:58,410 --> 00:20:02,410
We can go through and look at how we could implement this

298
00:20:02,410 --> 00:20:06,410
from scratch in a framework like TensorFlow,

299
00:20:06,410 --> 00:20:09,410
where we begin by initializing these weight matrices

300
00:20:09,410 --> 00:20:12,410
initializing a class for the RNN,

301
00:20:12,410 --> 00:20:15,410
and defining this forward pass computation

302
00:20:15,410 --> 00:20:18,410
and what we call the call function,

303
00:20:18,410 --> 00:20:21,410
which is effectively how you call the neural network.

304
00:20:21,410 --> 00:20:24,410
How do you make a forward pass going from input

305
00:20:24,410 --> 00:20:32,170
to prediction and using that prediction to get during training to compute a loss and update the loss.

306
00:20:32,170 --> 00:20:39,010
And so, again, to reiterate this computation, right, we update the hidden state,

307
00:20:39,010 --> 00:20:43,250
we compute the output and return both values back.

308
00:20:43,250 --> 00:20:49,250
In practice though, right, very rarely will you actually, or maybe almost never,

309
00:20:49,250 --> 00:20:53,690
build up a RNN unit from scratch.

310
00:20:53,690 --> 00:20:59,330
In fact, in both TensorFlow and PyTorch and common ML frameworks,

311
00:20:59,330 --> 00:21:05,530
there are existing implementations built in, where you can just use these RNN

312
00:21:05,530 --> 00:21:09,010
layers in practice in defining your neural network.

313
00:21:09,010 --> 00:21:16,530
And you'll get hands-on experience doing this in software lab 1, starting today.

314
00:21:16,530 --> 00:21:20,230
Okay, so to recap and put these elements together,

315
00:21:20,230 --> 00:21:23,310
we saw that with RNNs, we can,

316
00:21:23,310 --> 00:21:25,030
and sequence modeling in general,

317
00:21:25,030 --> 00:21:27,830
we have this idea of different types of tasks

318
00:21:27,830 --> 00:21:30,510
or problem formulations we can tackle.

319
00:21:30,510 --> 00:21:32,750
We can do classification tasks,

320
00:21:32,750 --> 00:21:36,550
we can do captioning tasks or text generation tasks,

321
00:21:36,550 --> 00:21:40,150
we can do sequence to sequence translation tasks.

322
00:21:40,150 --> 00:21:44,630
And that is really with a lot of these generative applications,

323
00:21:44,630 --> 00:21:50,830
like in language modeling, machine translation, natural language processing, many of these

324
00:21:50,830 --> 00:21:55,510
can be formulated according to these many to many problems.

325
00:21:55,510 --> 00:21:59,710
And you'll get hands-on experience with this.

326
00:21:59,710 --> 00:22:08,590
So to make this more concrete in terms of what this actually means in terms of a real

327
00:22:08,590 --> 00:22:12,390
use case or problem in the real world.

328
00:22:12,390 --> 00:22:17,270
I want to focus on a very, very pertinent sequence modeling

329
00:22:17,270 --> 00:22:21,950
problem, which is the basis for basically all language models,

330
00:22:21,950 --> 00:22:25,550
large language models, small language models that we see today,

331
00:22:25,550 --> 00:22:29,270
which is this notion of next word prediction.

332
00:22:29,270 --> 00:22:31,550
And we're going to use next word prediction

333
00:22:31,550 --> 00:22:36,910
to think about and work through core design criteria

334
00:22:36,910 --> 00:22:40,430
that we want to build out in any sort of neural network

335
00:22:40,430 --> 00:22:43,470
that we build for a sequence modeling problem.

336
00:22:43,470 --> 00:22:45,790
And this is a really important task,

337
00:22:45,790 --> 00:22:49,190
not only because of the relevance of next word prediction,

338
00:22:49,190 --> 00:22:52,430
but in general in thinking about your own deep learning

339
00:22:52,430 --> 00:22:56,990
applications or work or research that may be relevant to you,

340
00:22:56,990 --> 00:23:00,510
relating those problems back to design criteria

341
00:23:00,510 --> 00:23:02,910
in terms of the architectures you choose

342
00:23:02,910 --> 00:23:05,710
and the approach you actually take.

343
00:23:05,710 --> 00:23:12,910
So, let's say we have this sentence, this morning I took my cat for a walk.

344
00:23:12,910 --> 00:23:21,030
The task of next word prediction is given some set of words, let's say this morning I took

345
00:23:21,030 --> 00:23:26,710
my cat for a, predict the next word in the sentence.

346
00:23:26,710 --> 00:23:31,190
And so to do this, the very first thing we have to consider, whether we're using an

347
00:23:31,190 --> 00:23:33,790
and RNN or any other sort of neural network

348
00:23:33,790 --> 00:23:37,430
is how are we actually going to represent this data,

349
00:23:37,430 --> 00:23:39,530
language data, to the model.

350
00:23:40,950 --> 00:23:44,870
The way that we can formalize this is,

351
00:23:44,870 --> 00:23:48,790
let's say we have a model and we input the word deep

352
00:23:48,790 --> 00:23:50,910
and we want to use the neural network

353
00:23:50,910 --> 00:23:54,110
to predict the next word learning.

354
00:23:54,110 --> 00:23:56,030
What could be the issue here?

355
00:23:56,030 --> 00:23:59,510
If we're just trying to pass in words to the model,

356
00:23:59,510 --> 00:24:07,190
Will this actually work if not how can we make this work in terms of the data itself?

357
00:24:07,190 --> 00:24:10,190
Yes.

358
00:24:10,190 --> 00:24:11,510
You have to tokenize the words.

359
00:24:11,510 --> 00:24:26,660
Can you say more about what that means?

360
00:24:26,660 --> 00:24:27,660
Right.

361
00:24:27,660 --> 00:24:32,420
So it gets closer to this idea of can we embed something about the concept of the word

362
00:24:32,420 --> 00:24:35,980
to represent the model?

363
00:24:35,980 --> 00:24:42,060
It actually is a step earlier than that, which is we can't straight pass in, you know,

364
00:24:42,060 --> 00:24:47,500
characters to the model, they have to be represented numerically somehow, right?

365
00:24:47,500 --> 00:24:54,460
Because ultimately all these neural networks are, they're operating on numerical data.

366
00:24:54,460 --> 00:24:59,020
And so tokenization is one way that we can think about doing this.

367
00:24:59,020 --> 00:25:08,380
But regardless of what strategy we use, we have to process the language data to a numerical input.

368
00:25:08,380 --> 00:25:12,580
where we have a vector or an array or an index of numbers

369
00:25:12,580 --> 00:25:15,140
to the model that represents, let's say,

370
00:25:15,140 --> 00:25:20,140
the sequence of words and get a prediction of something

371
00:25:20,540 --> 00:25:24,940
that represents the next word but is in numerical form.

372
00:25:24,940 --> 00:25:27,740
And then we can map this back to the language space

373
00:25:27,740 --> 00:25:30,860
to actually get what the word prediction was.

374
00:25:30,860 --> 00:25:34,700
And so to formalize this, how do we actually do this?

375
00:25:34,700 --> 00:25:38,020
This gets to this idea that you raise in your answer

376
00:25:38,020 --> 00:25:47,020
transforming the words into some numerical representation and tokenization gives us one strategy to do that.

377
00:25:47,020 --> 00:25:53,020
So this is a concept of, in general, of an embedding or an encoding.

378
00:25:53,020 --> 00:26:00,020
And ideally we want this embedding to represent something about the semantic content of the words, right?

379
00:26:00,020 --> 00:26:07,460
right? Because language has this very rich structure, so something like learning and education

380
00:26:07,460 --> 00:26:14,340
maybe are mapped closer together than learning and tennis, right? So the approach for doing this

381
00:26:14,340 --> 00:26:21,060
is to consider the complete vocabulary or the corpus of words that are present in our language.

382
00:26:21,780 --> 00:26:29,940
And first start by mapping these words to individual unique indices. Then where do we go from

383
00:26:29,940 --> 00:26:37,820
there. A naive approach would be to just map an index to a fixed length vector where you

384
00:26:37,820 --> 00:26:46,540
have a single one-hot bit flip where that represents the identity of the word based on the index

385
00:26:46,540 --> 00:26:53,460
that's a one versus zero elsewhere. But this is not very informative, right? Because of

386
00:26:53,460 --> 00:26:58,260
the fact that we're not capturing anything about the semantics and this rich structure

387
00:26:58,260 --> 00:27:00,500
that's present in language.

388
00:27:00,500 --> 00:27:03,940
And so a more intelligent way to go about this

389
00:27:03,940 --> 00:27:06,940
is to leverage some sort of embedding scheme

390
00:27:06,940 --> 00:27:11,780
where there is capture of these semantic relationships.

391
00:27:11,780 --> 00:27:15,660
And so this is in general learning and embedding

392
00:27:15,660 --> 00:27:20,180
or learning a tokenization strategy to capture this.

393
00:27:20,180 --> 00:27:24,300
And in language models today, there are very popular schemes

394
00:27:24,300 --> 00:27:29,540
that basically effectively map chunks of words

395
00:27:29,540 --> 00:27:33,220
and chunks of language that occur frequently together

396
00:27:33,220 --> 00:27:36,620
to certain parts of this embedding space

397
00:27:36,620 --> 00:27:39,020
so that we can capture those relationships

398
00:27:39,020 --> 00:27:42,380
in terms of co-occurrence of particular characters

399
00:27:42,380 --> 00:27:46,860
or words or chunks in the vocabulary.

400
00:27:46,860 --> 00:27:48,940
So this is really the core idea.

401
00:27:48,940 --> 00:27:51,540
But the fundamental point that I want to convey

402
00:27:51,540 --> 00:27:54,860
is that in all cases, in all examples

403
00:27:54,860 --> 00:27:57,060
that we see throughout this class,

404
00:27:57,060 --> 00:28:00,380
neural networks operate on numerical inputs.

405
00:28:00,380 --> 00:28:02,620
So regardless of your data modality,

406
00:28:02,620 --> 00:28:05,700
you have to think about a way to map that data

407
00:28:05,700 --> 00:28:08,740
into numerical input to be processed

408
00:28:08,740 --> 00:28:13,740
and represented in a way that the neural network can handle.

409
00:28:14,820 --> 00:28:18,100
Okay, so that's design criteria number one, right?

410
00:28:18,100 --> 00:28:21,100
We need a way to map to numerical inputs,

411
00:28:21,100 --> 00:28:24,620
And ideally, we want this mapping to capture something

412
00:28:24,620 --> 00:28:27,940
about the structure of language.

413
00:28:27,940 --> 00:28:31,820
The second sort of design criteria that's critical for sequence

414
00:28:31,820 --> 00:28:34,940
modeling and this next word prediction task

415
00:28:34,940 --> 00:28:39,260
is being able to handle variable lengths of sequences.

416
00:28:39,260 --> 00:28:42,940
Short sentence, medium sentence, long sentence,

417
00:28:42,940 --> 00:28:46,020
doesn't matter, we should be able to operate over all

418
00:28:46,020 --> 00:28:50,050
of these types of inputs.

419
00:28:50,050 --> 00:28:53,130
Feed forward networks break down here, right?

420
00:28:53,130 --> 00:28:56,970
Because of the fact that there's a fixed number of inputs.

421
00:28:56,970 --> 00:29:00,490
But something like a state-based model like an RNN

422
00:29:00,490 --> 00:29:04,130
where we're processing the data time step by time step

423
00:29:04,130 --> 00:29:09,530
is inherently designed to process these variable length inputs.

424
00:29:09,530 --> 00:29:13,490
A final criteria that we want to think about

425
00:29:13,490 --> 00:29:17,090
is capturing these dependencies and relationships

426
00:29:17,090 --> 00:29:21,090
that can occur in sequence data like language.

427
00:29:21,090 --> 00:29:24,790
Oftentimes, there's really core information up front

428
00:29:24,790 --> 00:29:28,330
in the sentence that informs the next word prediction

429
00:29:28,330 --> 00:29:30,690
super downstream in the sentence.

430
00:29:30,690 --> 00:29:33,890
And we want to be able to capture this effectively.

431
00:29:34,890 --> 00:29:39,490
And finally, sequence data is sequential for a reason.

432
00:29:39,490 --> 00:29:42,290
That means that differences in the order

433
00:29:42,290 --> 00:29:45,530
can completely alter the, you know,

434
00:29:45,530 --> 00:29:49,730
what is actually represented in that sample.

435
00:29:49,730 --> 00:29:53,130
The food was good, not bad at all, means great.

436
00:29:53,130 --> 00:29:53,970
Good food.

437
00:29:53,970 --> 00:29:56,280
But if we flip the order of the words in the sentence,

438
00:29:56,280 --> 00:29:58,120
we've completely flipped the meaning.

439
00:29:58,120 --> 00:30:02,040
The exact words are the same, order matters.

440
00:30:02,040 --> 00:30:07,440
And so RNNs are one way to start to get at tackling

441
00:30:07,440 --> 00:30:11,240
these design criteria because they can handle variable length

442
00:30:11,240 --> 00:30:12,680
sequences.

443
00:30:12,680 --> 00:30:15,080
Through this notion of the internal state,

444
00:30:15,080 --> 00:30:18,240
we have some way of tracking and preserving

445
00:30:18,240 --> 00:30:20,760
information about these dependencies.

446
00:30:20,760 --> 00:30:25,320
We're processing the data step by step, so we have a notion of order.

447
00:30:25,320 --> 00:30:31,560
And we have this weight sharing that allows this build up of memory over time in the RNN

448
00:30:31,560 --> 00:30:35,780
to be able to capture those dependencies.

449
00:30:35,780 --> 00:30:46,780
Okay, so the final piece of RNN content that we'll talk about is how we actually train RNNs in practice.

450
00:30:46,780 --> 00:30:50,980
And that's based on the core neural network training algorithm

451
00:30:50,980 --> 00:30:55,020
that Alexander introduced which is back propagation.

452
00:30:55,020 --> 00:30:58,100
So let's do a refresher of how we actually

453
00:30:58,100 --> 00:31:00,620
train these feed forward models.

454
00:31:00,620 --> 00:31:04,580
We have an input, and we first make a forward pass

455
00:31:04,580 --> 00:31:07,500
through the network from input to output

456
00:31:07,500 --> 00:31:10,100
to generate a prediction.

457
00:31:10,100 --> 00:31:12,860
And then to actually compute the loss,

458
00:31:12,860 --> 00:31:19,020
update the parameters of the model, the weights in the model, we backpropagate gradients

459
00:31:19,020 --> 00:31:25,460
back through the network where the algorithm was to take the derivative of the loss with

460
00:31:25,460 --> 00:31:33,140
respect to the parameters or the weights in the network and then shifting the parameters

461
00:31:33,140 --> 00:31:37,500
in order to minimize this loss.

462
00:31:37,500 --> 00:31:43,500
Now, let's say we are going to extend this to the context of RNNs.

463
00:31:43,500 --> 00:31:48,380
As we saw with RNNs, we have this step-by-step sequential processing.

464
00:31:48,380 --> 00:31:54,940
So we can predict outputs at each time step, meaning we can get a loss at each time step,

465
00:31:54,940 --> 00:32:01,580
and we can get an aggregate loss by summing the losses at these individual time steps.

466
00:32:01,580 --> 00:32:07,460
Now in order to back propagate and make updates to the network, we need to do

467
00:32:07,460 --> 00:32:09,820
this over time as well.

468
00:32:09,820 --> 00:32:13,660
So instead of backpropagating error through a single feed

469
00:32:13,660 --> 00:32:17,700
forward network at a single time step, in RNNs,

470
00:32:17,700 --> 00:32:21,740
we backpropagate across these individual time steps

471
00:32:21,740 --> 00:32:25,900
and then across time steps from where we are currently back

472
00:32:25,900 --> 00:32:28,180
to the beginning of the sequence.

473
00:32:28,180 --> 00:32:32,100
And so this is the formulation of this algorithm

474
00:32:32,100 --> 00:32:36,540
for training RNNs called backpropagation through time

475
00:32:36,540 --> 00:32:41,420
because error is flowing back through time from where we are currently in the sequence

476
00:32:41,420 --> 00:32:46,700
back to the beginning time step one or time step zero.

477
00:32:46,700 --> 00:32:51,660
And so taking a closer look at how these gradients flow,

478
00:32:51,660 --> 00:32:57,300
we actually have to make these very repeated matrix multiplications

479
00:32:57,300 --> 00:33:05,300
with the weight matrix W to make because of these cell state updates requiring this passing

480
00:33:05,300 --> 00:33:08,140
of the weight matrix WH.

481
00:33:08,140 --> 00:33:10,940
And in practice, this can get a little tricky

482
00:33:10,940 --> 00:33:14,020
because computing the gradient back with respect

483
00:33:14,020 --> 00:33:17,460
to the state at the very first time step zero

484
00:33:17,460 --> 00:33:22,500
involves many, many repeated factors of WH of H

485
00:33:22,500 --> 00:33:25,260
and repeated gradient computations.

486
00:33:25,260 --> 00:33:29,620
In practice, if the values of this weight matrix are large,

487
00:33:29,620 --> 00:33:32,460
things can blow up very quickly, in problem

488
00:33:32,460 --> 00:33:34,260
that we call exploding gradients.

489
00:33:34,260 --> 00:33:38,060
And so often in practice, you'll actually clip the values

490
00:33:38,060 --> 00:33:42,100
of the gradients to some maximum so that you can scale

491
00:33:42,100 --> 00:33:44,100
back big gradients.

492
00:33:44,100 --> 00:33:46,380
You can also have the inverse problem

493
00:33:46,380 --> 00:33:50,140
where if the way matrix ends up being very small,

494
00:33:50,140 --> 00:33:52,980
you have repeated multiplications of something

495
00:33:52,980 --> 00:33:54,780
very small and things shrink.

496
00:33:54,780 --> 00:33:58,620
And then you get very, very little signal to actually learn

497
00:33:58,620 --> 00:33:59,500
on.

498
00:33:59,500 --> 00:34:03,300
And this is known as the vanishing gradient problem.

499
00:34:03,300 --> 00:34:10,020
And there are as well practical tricks in terms of solutions and

500
00:34:10,020 --> 00:34:14,840
implementations that people use to mitigate this problem.

501
00:34:14,840 --> 00:34:15,600
Yes, questions?

502
00:34:15,600 --> 00:34:25,750
So each zero is neither an explicit parameter nor an input.

503
00:34:25,750 --> 00:34:30,670
Right, it's this internal state variable, if you will.

504
00:34:30,670 --> 00:34:36,670
And it is dependent on the weight matrix parameter and the input.

505
00:34:36,670 --> 00:34:48,370
So if you think about the input as being a variable, H0 is a variable that depends on computation involving the weight matrices W.

506
00:34:48,370 --> 00:35:01,370
Okay. So in practice, right, this vanishing gradient problem has real implications in particular with respect to our ability to track long term dependencies in the data.

507
00:35:02,370 --> 00:35:08,290
And I won't spend too much time on this, but just to give you a sense, when we multiply

508
00:35:08,290 --> 00:35:16,530
many of these small gradient values or matrix values together, it biases the model to try

509
00:35:16,530 --> 00:35:23,810
to capture the shorter term things, because further back you're going to have more propagation

510
00:35:23,810 --> 00:35:27,610
of error and more of this vanishing problem.

511
00:35:27,610 --> 00:35:34,930
And so while this allows for effective capture of dependencies that are relatively short-term,

512
00:35:34,930 --> 00:35:41,410
it poses a problem for important longer-term dependencies where the input length can grow

513
00:35:41,410 --> 00:35:48,010
very long in terms of number of time steps and becomes problematic for the RNN to actually

514
00:35:48,010 --> 00:35:52,370
keep track of these longer-term dependencies.

515
00:35:52,370 --> 00:36:00,370
So in practice, there has been a lot of architecture development around these RNN style models.

516
00:36:00,370 --> 00:36:06,770
And in particular, there's this notion of making the internal computation of the RNN more

517
00:36:06,770 --> 00:36:14,690
complex to better control the flow of information and the state update equation itself.

518
00:36:14,690 --> 00:36:22,770
And one really pioneering solution to this was many years ago, a group led by Yurgen Schmitt

519
00:36:22,770 --> 00:36:23,770
Hubert.

520
00:36:23,770 --> 00:36:31,250
I introduced this mechanism called Long Short-term Memory that was really a foundational

521
00:36:31,250 --> 00:36:39,850
advance in terms of better controlling for these state updates in architectures like RNNs.

