00:38:13,810 --> 00:38:20,810
and hopefully give you enough of a teaser to motivate entries into software lab 1 competition.

538
00:38:20,810 --> 00:38:28,810
Okay, so in the final 15 minutes or so, we're going to move beyond recurrent models and think really about

539
00:38:28,810 --> 00:38:37,810
taking a step back to, again, the design criteria and capabilities of that we desire with sequence modeling.

540
00:38:37,810 --> 00:38:41,850
And thinking about what are the limitations of RNNs

541
00:38:41,850 --> 00:38:43,170
from a technical perspective

542
00:38:43,170 --> 00:38:46,010
and how this has motivated new advances

543
00:38:46,010 --> 00:38:49,290
in architectures for a sequence modeling.

544
00:38:49,290 --> 00:38:52,650
So the first fundamental limitation of RNNs

545
00:38:52,650 --> 00:38:55,770
is that they have this encoding bottleneck

546
00:38:55,770 --> 00:38:59,090
that's imposed by this notion of the internal state,

547
00:38:59,090 --> 00:39:01,090
H of T, this variable.

548
00:39:01,090 --> 00:39:03,530
We are trying to pack as much information

549
00:39:03,530 --> 00:39:07,290
about the dependencies and the structure of the data

550
00:39:07,290 --> 00:39:14,330
into that internal state, HFT, and use this to carry forward time step by step.

551
00:39:14,330 --> 00:39:19,730
Relatedly, right, when we process the data time step by time step, this can be pretty

552
00:39:19,730 --> 00:39:20,730
slow.

553
00:39:20,730 --> 00:39:27,210
We have to take the individual inputs, the individual slices as individual slices.

554
00:39:27,210 --> 00:39:30,330
And so it's not really paralyzable.

555
00:39:30,330 --> 00:39:36,530
And as a result of these two elements, especially that encoding bottleneck, the RNN can really

556
00:39:36,530 --> 00:39:44,370
quickly run into memory issues in terms of how long of a dependency is it able to capture.

557
00:39:44,370 --> 00:39:50,970
And so if we go back again to our fundamental goal of sequence modeling, we want to take

558
00:39:50,970 --> 00:39:56,480
a input that's represented as a sequence and learn some set of features

559
00:39:57,120 --> 00:40:04,720
that then map to a sequence of outputs. And RNNs use this notion of a recurrence relation and

560
00:40:04,720 --> 00:40:13,200
this step-by-step modeling to try to track these dependencies. But they have these three key bottlenecks

561
00:40:13,200 --> 00:40:19,520
that I mentioned. Ideally, we don't have that encoding bottleneck and we're able to look

562
00:40:19,520 --> 00:40:27,040
at this sequence globally as a continuous stream of information, but still able to capture

563
00:40:27,040 --> 00:40:34,320
local relationships and dependencies in the data. Ideally, we don't have to handle the sequence

564
00:40:34,320 --> 00:40:40,800
time step by time step. We're able to parallelize very efficiently so that we can handle long

565
00:40:40,800 --> 00:40:48,520
bodies of input. And we want to have this notion of long memory. And so with that experiment

566
00:40:48,520 --> 00:40:55,520
and a question is, what if we eliminated the need for recurrence entirely? How could

567
00:40:55,520 --> 00:41:04,440
we possibly approach this? One naive approach would be to kind of squash everything together.

568
00:41:04,440 --> 00:41:12,600
take one vector input and take the data from all the time points, concatenate them as

569
00:41:12,600 --> 00:41:16,400
one input vector, a single input.

570
00:41:16,400 --> 00:41:22,080
And feed this into the model and calculate some feature vector and use that to generate

571
00:41:22,080 --> 00:41:27,160
an output which we hope makes sense and is sensible.

572
00:41:27,160 --> 00:41:33,680
But yes, while we've eliminated this recurrence, we've eliminated this step by step processing

573
00:41:33,680 --> 00:41:36,840
this is not going to be scalable, right?

574
00:41:36,840 --> 00:41:40,600
Because if we were to build out a dense network to do this,

575
00:41:40,600 --> 00:41:45,880
this quickly gets very, very large as the sequence length grows.

576
00:41:45,880 --> 00:41:48,960
Importantly, we've lost the order information

577
00:41:48,960 --> 00:41:52,280
because those dependencies are gone.

578
00:41:52,280 --> 00:41:55,560
And because of that, there's no temporal dependency,

579
00:41:55,560 --> 00:41:58,920
so how can we really build out memory?

580
00:41:58,920 --> 00:42:01,760
And so that's because when we have

581
00:42:01,760 --> 00:42:07,840
fully connected or concatenated inputs. There's no notion of how do we actually pick out and

582
00:42:07,840 --> 00:42:15,280
attend to the parts in that input that are going to be important. And so several years ago now there

583
00:42:15,280 --> 00:42:23,120
was this breakthrough idea in this field of this notion of can we devise mathematically a way for

584
00:42:23,120 --> 00:42:30,560
the network to learn how to pick up and identify those dependencies locally and also globally that

585
00:42:30,560 --> 00:42:34,080
that are going to be important in the sequence overall.

586
00:42:34,080 --> 00:42:36,680
And this is the notion of attention.

587
00:42:36,680 --> 00:42:39,520
And this is a very powerful mechanism

588
00:42:39,520 --> 00:42:43,160
for learning in sequence modeling applications

589
00:42:43,160 --> 00:42:47,800
and is really the backbone of the transformer architecture.

590
00:42:47,800 --> 00:42:51,880
So if you know GPT or other models like BERT,

591
00:42:51,880 --> 00:42:56,040
the T in all of these acronyms stands for transformer.

592
00:42:56,040 --> 00:43:00,200
And the foundational mechanism behind these transformers

593
00:43:00,200 --> 00:43:02,800
is the attention operation.

594
00:43:02,800 --> 00:43:07,280
And to me, a very, very elegant and intuitive idea

595
00:43:07,280 --> 00:43:08,640
and we're going to break it down

596
00:43:08,640 --> 00:43:11,000
both from the intuition point of view

597
00:43:11,000 --> 00:43:13,960
and also the math to show you how that works.

598
00:43:15,160 --> 00:43:18,800
So the core intuition behind attention

599
00:43:18,800 --> 00:43:22,800
is can the neural network learn to identify

600
00:43:22,800 --> 00:43:26,000
what parts of an input within itself

601
00:43:26,000 --> 00:43:29,760
are most important to representing that input?

602
00:43:29,760 --> 00:43:36,960
And I think it's often easier to start with a visual example rather than a text example.

