00:48:41,040 --> 00:48:48,760
an individual sequence input, can we identify those core relationships in that sequence input

667
00:48:48,760 --> 00:48:51,410
itself?

668
00:48:51,410 --> 00:48:59,850
To extract that, we're going to do three transformations of this embedding to get our query, our key, and our value.

669
00:48:59,850 --> 00:49:11,810
The first is a query, and it's going to be done by a learned layer that transforms the positional embedding into a query matrix.

670
00:49:11,810 --> 00:49:17,690
And similarly, we will have another learned layer for the key matrix, and

671
00:49:17,690 --> 00:49:21,690
third learned layer for the value matrix.

672
00:49:21,690 --> 00:49:27,450
Importantly note that the positional embedding is the same for all these three.

673
00:49:27,450 --> 00:49:33,290
But those three layers, and therefore the three transformed outputs of query key and

674
00:49:33,290 --> 00:49:36,890
value are going to be different, capturing different information.

675
00:49:38,650 --> 00:49:42,650
Now we need to actually compute the attention score.

676
00:49:42,650 --> 00:49:46,090
How similar is that query to the key?

677
00:49:46,090 --> 00:49:48,090
How can we identify those most?

678
00:49:48,090 --> 00:49:50,120
important parts?

679
00:49:50,120 --> 00:49:54,160
The way we can do this is to compute a mathematical similarity

680
00:49:54,160 --> 00:49:57,400
between these two matrices.

681
00:49:57,400 --> 00:50:02,460
And mathematically, we can do that through an operation

682
00:50:02,460 --> 00:50:06,200
simply that is related to, based on the dot product.

683
00:50:06,200 --> 00:50:09,320
Right? So if we have two vectors in space,

684
00:50:09,320 --> 00:50:12,040
let's say we represent the query vector in blue

685
00:50:12,040 --> 00:50:16,320
and the key vector in orange, using the dot product,

686
00:50:16,320 --> 00:50:20,880
we can compute how aligned they are in space.

687
00:50:20,880 --> 00:50:23,760
And so we take the dot product between the query

688
00:50:23,760 --> 00:50:27,000
and the key and scale it to get this notion

689
00:50:27,000 --> 00:50:29,280
of a similarity metric.

690
00:50:29,280 --> 00:50:32,880
And this is also known as the cosine similarity, right?

691
00:50:34,280 --> 00:50:36,160
And so fundamentally, right,

692
00:50:36,160 --> 00:50:40,160
we can apply the same operation to matrices,

693
00:50:40,160 --> 00:50:46,960
this dot product and this scaling to get us this similarity metric.

694
00:50:46,960 --> 00:50:53,400
If we visualize what the result of this operation could mean, remember we're trying to compute

695
00:50:53,400 --> 00:50:59,600
self-attention, meaning within the sequence he tossed the tennis ball to serve, what are

696
00:50:59,600 --> 00:51:06,160
the components of that sequence that relate more strongly to each other versus others?

697
00:51:06,160 --> 00:51:13,480
If we were to learn attention over this compute attention score over this input, we could look

698
00:51:13,480 --> 00:51:20,480
like we could get something similar to this, where in this matrix we for each position

699
00:51:20,480 --> 00:51:22,880
right we compute a score.

700
00:51:22,880 --> 00:51:28,040
Along the diagonal is going to have the highest relation because the words are the same.

701
00:51:28,040 --> 00:51:34,480
But the words that are more semantically related and relationally related should also have

702
00:51:34,480 --> 00:51:39,600
high scores, for example, between ball and toss.

703
00:51:39,600 --> 00:51:45,240
And so when we compute this stop product of the querying the key, apply the scaling.

704
00:51:45,240 --> 00:51:52,000
We finally apply a function, the softmax, to squash these values between zero and one.

705
00:51:52,000 --> 00:51:54,840
And this gives us our attention weight.

706
00:51:54,840 --> 00:52:02,240
And when you look at the entries of an attention matrix, this gives you a notion of the relative

707
00:52:02,240 --> 00:52:08,560
importance of parts of that sequence of input relative to each other.

708
00:52:08,560 --> 00:52:14,760
And this attention-weighting is finally what we use to actually extract the features out

709
00:52:14,760 --> 00:52:21,240
by multiplying this with the value to get the final output.

710
00:52:21,240 --> 00:52:26,840
So putting this all together, right, the core idea of using this attention-weighting to

711
00:52:26,840 --> 00:52:30,180
to identify the parts of importance,

712
00:52:30,180 --> 00:52:34,240
multiplying it with the value to extract those features,

713
00:52:34,240 --> 00:52:37,240
puts us in a great place to now bring this forward,

714
00:52:37,240 --> 00:52:40,960
this attention mechanism as a part of a neural network.

715
00:52:40,960 --> 00:52:43,040
So to recap, our goal again,

716
00:52:43,040 --> 00:52:46,160
identify and attend to the most important features

717
00:52:46,160 --> 00:52:48,160
in a sequence input.

718
00:52:48,160 --> 00:52:50,500
Architecturally, what this looks like

719
00:52:50,500 --> 00:52:53,400
is we take the sequence positional encoding,

720
00:52:53,400 --> 00:52:55,280
we have three copies.

721
00:52:55,280 --> 00:53:03,200
We have three learned neural network layers that allow for transformation of these encodings

722
00:53:03,200 --> 00:53:06,400
to the query key in value.

723
00:53:06,400 --> 00:53:13,000
We can use this to compute the similarity score by computing these attention weights between

724
00:53:13,000 --> 00:53:20,800
the query and the key, scaling it, applying the softmax to get values between 0 and 1,

725
00:53:20,800 --> 00:53:26,120
And finally, extracting the features with high attention via the multiplication with the

726
00:53:26,120 --> 00:53:29,080
value matrix.

727
00:53:29,080 --> 00:53:34,880
And so together, this operations that I'm showing in the right are the basis of what we call

728
00:53:34,880 --> 00:53:40,680
an attention head that can plug into a larger network architecture.

729
00:53:40,680 --> 00:53:47,640
And so modern transformers use this attention operation to compute this very similar type

730
00:53:47,640 --> 00:53:54,600
of relation to learn these patterns of importance in sequential data.

731
00:53:54,600 --> 00:53:59,960
And this is really the core building block of the transformer architecture.

732
00:53:59,960 --> 00:54:04,640
What's really powerful is that it's very, very parallelizable.

733
00:54:04,640 --> 00:54:10,960
And we can also put together multiple attention heads, stacking them together within one network

734
00:54:10,960 --> 00:54:15,440
to identify and learn different patterns of importance.

735
00:54:15,440 --> 00:54:22,000
And so you can think of this intuitively as maybe we have multiple attention heads in the

736
00:54:22,000 --> 00:54:28,240
network where visually we can pick out Ironman but we can also pick out this building that

737
00:54:28,240 --> 00:54:33,080
you know he's hopefully not going to crash into and this alien spaceship looming in the

738
00:54:33,080 --> 00:54:34,080
background.

739
00:54:34,080 --> 00:54:35,080
Right.

740
00:54:35,080 --> 00:54:41,040
So this is just very I want to emphasize that this is an intuitive example to ground you but

741
00:54:41,040 --> 00:54:45,540
conceptually this idea of stacking these attention heads is very,

742
00:54:45,540 --> 00:54:49,540
very practical in terms of building out these transformer architectures.

743
00:54:50,640 --> 00:54:58,340
So today it's truly remarkable how far this architecture has come in terms of

744
00:54:58,340 --> 00:55:03,340
the capabilities that we have seen as a result of not only that innovation,

745
00:55:03,340 --> 00:55:05,340
but many innovations that have followed.

746
00:55:05,340 --> 00:55:14,340
And it's not just in natural language and transformer LLM's like GPT, but also in other domains as well.

747
00:55:14,340 --> 00:55:25,340
In biology, in thinking about sequences like proteins or DNA, and how we can learn relationships using transformer-based architectures,

748
